rn { data: JSON.parse(cached), isStale: true };\n    throw err;\n  }\n}","starter_code":"async function fetchWithFallback(liveFetchFn, cacheKey) {\n  // TODO: try live fetch, catch error and return cached fallback\n}","title":"Exercise 2: Fallback Cache Handler for Failing Services"}],"objectives":["Decompose monolithic architectures into autonomous services using Domain-Driven Design (DDD) bounded contexts.","Implement API Gateway routing, centralized token verification, and rate limiting.","Design asynchronous event-driven messaging pipelines using message queues to decouple services.","Implement resilience patterns: Circuit Breakers, Exponential Backoff, and Distributed Tracing (OpenTelemetry)."],"outcome":"Confidence in decomposing monoliths into resilient, decoupled microservices suited for high-availability environments.","project":"Distributed Multi-Tier Microservices Platform with API Gateway, Order Processing & Circuit Breaker","quiz":[{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"In the Database-per-Service pattern, each microservice encapsulates its own persistent store. Other services must interact solely through published public APIs, preventing tight database coupling and enabling independent deployment and scaling.","id":1,"options":["Every microservice must use a different database engine (e.g. MySQL, MongoDB, Redis)","Each microservice owns its own private database; no other service may directly query its tables, ensuring loose coupling and independent schema evolution","All microservices connect to one shared database table to maximize join performance","Databases must be rebooted every time a service restarts"],"question":"What is the \"Database-per-Service\" pattern in microservices architecture, and why is it essential?"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Closed passes requests through normally; Open fails fast immediately to prevent cascading crashes when failure thresholds are exceeded; Half-Open allows limited probe requests through after a timeout to check if the upstream service has recovered.","id":2,"options":["Active, Inactive, Deprecated","Closed (normal operations), Open (failing fast without calling upstream), and Half-Open (trial requests to test recovery)","Synchronous, Asynchronous, Queued","Primary, Secondary, Standby"],"question":"What are the three operational states of a Circuit Breaker pattern?"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"The Saga pattern achieves eventual consistency across services by executing local transactions. If any step fails, the saga executes compensating transactions that undo the changes made by preceding steps.","id":3,"options":["By running all transactions in a single global database transaction","By executing a sequence of local transactions where each step updates its local DB, publishing events that trigger the next step, and executing compensating transactions if a step fails","By ignoring failures and treating all distributed writes as fire-and-forget","By taking hourly database snapshots"],"question":"How does the Saga pattern maintain data consistency across distributed microservice databases without distributed 2-Phase Commit (2PC) locks?"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"An API Gateway shields clients from internal microservices decomposition by providing a unified facade. It routes requests, terminates SSL, validates authentication tokens, and applies rate limiting.","id":4,"options":["It acts as the single entry point for clients, routing requests to internal services, and handling cross-cutting concerns like authentication, SSL termination, and rate limiting","It compiles frontend React JSX files on the server","It serves as the main SQL database driver","It manages local git repositories"],"question":"What is the role of an API Gateway in a microservices architecture?"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"gRPC uses binary Protocol Buffers serialization rather than verbose text JSON, resulting in much smaller payloads and faster serialization. Running over HTTP/2, it supports multiplexed streams and bidirectional streaming.","id":5,"options":["Protocol Buffers serialize binary data with significantly smaller payloads, lower CPU overhead, and compile-time type safety over HTTP/2 multiplexed streams","gRPC does not require a network connection","REST APIs cannot be run inside Docker containers","gRPC is an official requirement of the W3C standard"],"question":"Why is gRPC (using Protocol Buffers) often preferred over REST/JSON for internal inter-service communication between microservices?"}],"resources":[{"description":"The definitive catalog of microservices architecture patterns by Chris Richardson.","name":"Microservices.io Pattern Language","url":"https://microservices.io/patterns/"},{"description":"Deep architectural overview of handling transient faults and circuit states.","name":"Microsoft Cloud Design Patterns: Circuit Breaker","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/architecture/patterns/circuit-breaker"},{"description":"Standards for tracing distributed HTTP requests across microservice boundaries.","name":"OpenTelemetry Distributed Tracing","url":"https://opentelemetry.io/docs/concepts/signals/traces/"}],"syllabus":[{"code_snippet":"// Domain Boundaries in CareerSense AI:\n// 1. Auth Service: User credentials, JWT issuance, profile ownership\n// 2. Resume Intelligence Service: PDF extraction, NLP embeddings, score calculation\n// 3. Job Match Service: Job description ingestion, TF-IDF cosine comparison\n// 4. Learning Roadmap Service: Personalized milestones, task tracking, quiz grading","deep_dive":"Microservices must be autonomous and independently deployable. The cardinal rule of microservices is the Database-per-Service pattern: no service should directly query another service's database.","key_concepts":["Domain-Driven Design (DDD)","Bounded Contexts","Database-per-Service Pattern","Strangler Fig Pattern"],"module_title":"Module 1: Monolith Decomposition & Bounded Contexts"},{"code_snippet":"// Reverse Proxy / API Gateway Routing Concept (Node.js)\nconst express = require('express');\nconst { createProxyMiddleware } = require('http-proxy-middleware');\n\nconst app = express();\n\n// Route to Auth Service\napp.use('/api/auth', createProxyMiddleware({ target: 'http://auth-service:5001', changeOrigin: true }));\n\n// Route to Resume Analysis Service\napp.use('/api/resume', createProxyMiddleware({ target: 'http://resume-service:5002', changeOrigin: true }));\n\n// Route to Career Roadmap Service\napp.use('/api/career-roadmap', createProxyMiddleware({ target: 'http://roadmap-service:5003', changeOrigin: true }));\n\napp.listen(8080, () => console.log('API Gateway running on port 8080'));","deep_dive":"An API Gateway sits between external clients and internal microservices. It handles client authentication, SSL termination, and rate limiting, routing requests to internal private network endpoints.","key_concepts":["API Gateway Responsibilities","Synchronous HTTP/REST vs gRPC Protobuf","Request Routing & Load Balancing","Cross-Cutting Concerns (Auth, Rate Limits)"],"module_title":"Module 2: API Gateway & Inter-Service Communication"},{"code_snippet":"// Event-Driven Decoupled Workflow\n// 1. Candidate uploads resume -> Resume Service stores PDF and emits 'resume.uploaded'\n// 2. Job Match Service consumes 'resume.uploaded' and re-computes similarity match\n// 3. Roadmap Service consumes 'resume.analyzed' and generates personalized milestones\n// 4. Notification Service consumes 'roadmap.ready' and emails the candidate","deep_dive":"Distributed transactions spanning multiple databases cannot use traditional ACID locks without severe performance bottlenecks. The Saga pattern executes a sequence of local transactions, triggering compensating transactions on failure.","key_concepts":["Event-Driven Architecture","Message Brokers (RabbitMQ / Kafka / Redis Streams)","Eventual Consistency vs ACID","Saga Orchestration vs Choreography"],"module_title":"Module 3: Asynchronous Messaging & The Saga Pattern"},{"code_snippet":"// Circuit Breaker State Machine Concept\nclass CircuitBreaker {\n  constructor(failureThreshold = 5, cooldownPeriodMs = 10000) {\n    this.failureThreshold = failureThreshold;\n    this.cooldownPeriodMs = cooldownPeriodMs;\n    this.failureCount = 0;\n    this.state = 'CLOSED'; // CLOSED, OPEN, HALF_OPEN\n    this.nextAttempt = Date.now();\n  }\n\n  async execute(action) {\n    if (this.state === 'OPEN') {\n      if (Date.now() > this.nextAttempt) {\n        this.state = 'HALF_OPEN';\n      } else {\n        throw new Error('Circuit Breaker OPEN: Service temporarily unavailable');\n      }\n    }\n\n    try {\n      const result = await action();\n      this.reset();\n      return result;\n    } catch (err) {\n      this.recordFailure();\n      throw err;\n    }\n  }\n\n  recordFailure() {\n    this.failureCount++;\n    if (this.failureCount >= this.failureThreshold) {\n      this.state = 'OPEN';\n      this.nextAttempt = Date.now() + this.cooldownPeriodMs;\n      console.warn('Circuit breaker tripped to OPEN state');\n    }\n  }\n\n  reset() {\n    this.failureCount = 0;\n    this.state = 'CLOSED';\n  }\n}","deep_dive":"When an upstream service slows or crashes, calling services can exhaust their thread pools waiting for timeouts, collapsing the entire system. A Circuit Breaker trips to OPEN after consecutive errors, failing fast without hitting the dying service.","key_concepts":["Cascading Failures","Circuit Breaker States (Closed, Open, Half-Open)","Exponential Backoff with Jitter","OpenTelemetry Tracing Headers"],"module_title":"Module 4: Resilience & The Circuit Breaker Pattern"}],"title":"Microservices Architecture & Distributed Systems","topics":["Monolith Decomposition & Bounded Contexts (DDD)","API Gateway Pattern & Service Communication (REST vs gRPC)","Asynchronous Messaging & Eventual Consistency (Saga Pattern)","Resilience Patterns: Circuit Breakers, Retries & Distributed Tracing"]},"duration_weeks":"Week 2 (