s responsibility when using Amazon EC2?"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Attaching an IAM Role to an EC2 instance allows AWS to automatically rotate and issue temporary security credentials to the instance, eliminating the catastrophic risk of accidentally leaking hardcoded API keys on GitHub.","id":2,"options":["IAM Roles do not support permissions","IAM Roles provide temporary rotating credentials automatically via instance metadata without storing sensitive keys in code or files","IAM Roles are twice as fast as IAM Users","IAM Users cannot connect to S3"],"question":"Why are IAM Roles preferred over hardcoded IAM User Access Keys when granting EC2 instances access to Amazon S3?"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Security Groups are stateful firewalls operating at the instance/ENI level (if inbound traffic is allowed, corresponding outbound response traffic is automatically permitted). NACLs are stateless rules evaluated at the subnet boundary.","id":3,"options":["Security Groups are stateful (inbound traffic response is automatically allowed outbound); NACLs are stateless and operate at the subnet level","Security Groups only work on Windows servers","NACLs are managed by Google Cloud","There is no difference between them"],"question":"What is the primary architectural difference between an AWS Security Group and a Network ACL (NACL)?"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Pre-signed URLs include authentication query parameters generated using the server's IAM credentials, allowing an unauthenticated client to upload or download only the specified object for a strict time window.","id":4,"options":["It permanently changes bucket permissions to public for 5 minutes","It encodes a temporary cryptographic signature using server IAM credentials that grants access only to a specific object key for a limited expiration window","It bypasses Amazon S3 security policies entirely","It requires the user to log in with an AWS root account password"],"question":"How does an S3 Pre-Signed URL allow secure client uploads without making the entire bucket publicly readable/writable?"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Database instances should always reside in private subnets with no public internet routing. Security groups must restrict database access solely to backend application servers located within the authorized VPC.","id":5,"options":["In a public subnet with an open 0.0.0.0/0 security group rule on port 3306","In a private subnet with no public IP address, accessible only by security group rules permitting traffic from the EC2 backend server","Directly on the client user's mobile phone","On an unencrypted FTP server"],"question":"Where should you place your Amazon RDS database instance in a secure VPC architecture?"}],"resources":[{"description":"Free interactive learning courses and cloud architecture pathways.","name":"AWS Skill Builder Official Modules","url":"https://skillbuilder.aws/"},{"description":"Architectural best practices for security, reliability, performance, and cost.","name":"AWS Well-Architected Framework","url":"https://aws.amazon.com/architecture/well-architected/"},{"description":"Complete guide to launching, configuring, and connecting to EC2 instances.","name":"Amazon EC2 Linux User Guide","url":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AWSEC2/latest/UserGuide/"}],"syllabus":[{"code_snippet":"// Least-Privilege IAM Policy for S3 Resume Bucket\n{\n  \"Version\": \"2012-10-17\",\n  \"Statement\": [\n    {\n      \"Sid\": \"AllowS3ResumeUploads\",\n      \"Effect\": \"Allow\",\n      \"Action\": [\n        \"s3:PutObject\",\n        \"s3:GetObject\"\n      ],\n      \"Resource\": \"arn:aws:s3:::careersense-resumes-prod/*\"\n    }\n  ]\n}","deep_dive":"Never use the AWS root account for daily operations. Create IAM roles with tightly scoped JSON policy permissions and attach them directly to EC2 instances to avoid baking static API keys into code.","key_concepts":["AWS Shared Responsibility Model","IAM Users, Groups & Roles","Least Privilege Principle","Security Groups vs Network ACLs"],"module_title":"Module 1: Cloud Security Fundamentals & IAM"},{"code_snippet":"# NGINX Configuration on EC2 (/etc/nginx/sites-available/careersense)\nserver {\n    listen 80;\n    server_name api.careersense.ai;\n\n    location / {\n        proxy_pass http://127.0.0.1:5000;\n        proxy_http_version 1.1;\n        proxy_set_header Upgrade $http_upgrade;\n        proxy_set_header Connection 'upgrade';\n        proxy_set_header Host $host;\n        proxy_set_header X-Real-IP $remote_addr;\n        proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-For $proxy_add_x_forwarded_for;\n        proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-Proto $scheme;\n    }\n}","deep_dive":"EC2 provides resizable compute capacity in the cloud. We configure an Ubuntu instance, lock down Security Groups to port 80/443 (HTTP/HTTPS) and port 22 (SSH), and run NGINX to reverse-proxy traffic to backend node processes.","key_concepts":["AMI Selection (Ubuntu 22.04 LTS)","Instance Types (t3.micro / t3.small)","SSH Keypair Authentication","NGINX Reverse Proxy & Certbot Let's Encrypt"],"module_title":"Module 2: Amazon EC2 Compute & Production Host Configuration"},{"code_snippet":"# Generating an S3 Pre-Signed Upload URL in Python (Boto3)\nimport boto3\nfrom botocore.config import Config\n\ns3_client = boto3.client('s3', region_name='us-east-1', config=Config(signature_version='s3v4'))\n\ndef generate_presigned_upload_url(bucket_name, object_key, expires_in=300):\n    url = s3_client.generate_presigned_url(\n        'put_object',\n        Params={'Bucket': bucket_name, 'Key': object_key, 'ContentType': 'application/pdf'},\n        ExpiresIn=expires_in\n    )\n    return url","deep_dive":"Storing user resumes or PDFs on local EC2 disk is brittle (lost when instances terminate). Storing assets in Amazon S3 provides 99.999999999% (11 9s) durability. CloudFront caches assets at edge locations worldwide.","key_concepts":["S3 Buckets & Object Storage","Pre-Signed URLs for Secure Client Uploads","Block Public Access Configuration","CloudFront Low-Latency Edge Caching"],"module_title":"Module 3: Amazon S3 & CloudFront Global CDN"},{"code_snippet":"# Secure Cloud Production Environment Variables (.env)\nDATABASE_URL=mysql+pymysql://careersense_app:SecureRDSSecret@careersense-db.c9k4x.us-east-1.rds.amazonaws.com:3306/careersense_ai\nAWS_STORAGE_BUCKET_NAME=careersense-resumes-prod\nAWS_DEFAULT_REGION=us-east-1\nCDN_DOMAIN=https://d123456abcdef8.cloudfront.net","deep_dive":"Amazon RDS automates hardware provisioning, database setup, patching, and backups. Placing RDS in private VPC subnets ensures database ports (3306) are never directly exposed to the public internet.","key_concepts":["Managed MySQL Instance Provisioning","Multi-AZ Deployments for High Availability","Automated Backups & Point-in-Time Recovery","VPC Private Subnet Security"],"module_title":"Module 4: Amazon RDS Managed Databases & Cloud Architecture"}],"title":"AWS Cloud Architecture & Scalable Deployment","topics":["IAM Roles, Least-Privilege Policies & Security Groups","Amazon EC2 Compute Instances, SSH Keys & NGINX Reverse Proxy","Amazon S3 Object Storage, Bucket Policies & CloudFront CDN","Amazon RDS Managed MySQL Databases & Cloud Production Deployment"]},"duration_weeks":"Week 4 (Sprint)","expected_outcome":"Proficiency in configuring scalable AWS cloud infrastructure, security groups, and automated production deployment.","id":568,"order_index":6,"phase_number":6,"practice_project":"Deploy CareerSense AI Full-Stack Application on AWS EC2 with S3 File Storage & Amazon RDS MySQL","priority":"HIGH","progress":0,"resource_categories":[{"description":"Free interactive learning courses and cloud architecture pathways.","name":"AWS Skill Builder Official Modules","url":"https://skillbuilder.aws/"},{"description":"Architectural best practices for security, reliability, performance, and cost.","name":"AWS Well-Architected Framework","url":"https://aws.amazon.com/architecture/well-architected/"},{"description":"Complete guide to launching, configuring, and connecting to EC2 instances.","name":"Amazon EC2 Linux User Guide","url":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AWSEC2/latest/UserGuide/"}],"roadmap_id":37,"skill_name":"AWS","status":"Not Started","tasks":[{"allocated_hours":1.0,"day":1,"description":"Study core architecture, terminology, and foundational concepts of AWS.","is_completed":false,"status":"Not Started","task_title":"Core Fundamentals & Concept Overview","title":"Day 1: Core Fundamentals & Concept Overview","week":4},{"allocated_hours":1.0,"day":2,"description":"Configure local development environment, tooling, and run first working AWS example.","is_completed":false,"status":"Not Started","task_title":"Environment Setup & Hello-World","title":"Day 2: Environment Setup & Hello-World","week":4},{"allocated_hours":1.0,"day":3,"description":"Practice key syntax patterns, methods, and configurations in AWS.","is_completed":false,"status":"Not Started","task_title":"Syntax & Essential APIs","title":"Day 3: Syntax & Essential APIs","week":4},{"allocated_hours":1.0,"day":4,"description":"Complete guided coding exercises implementing IAM Roles, Least-Privilege Policies & Security Groups.","is_completed":false,"status":"Not Started","task_title":"Hands-on Coding Exercises","title":"Day 4: Hands-on Coding Exercises","week":4},{"allocated_hours":1.0,"day":5,"description":"Connect AWS with an external library or data source and test functionality.","is_completed":false,"status":"Not Started","task_title":"Mini Integration Lab","title":"Day 5: Mini Integration Lab","week":4}],"title":"Phase 6: AWS Cloud Architecture & Scalable Deployment","topics":["IAM Roles, Least-Privilege Policies & Security Groups","Amazon EC2 Compute Instances, SSH Keys & NGINX Reverse Proxy","Amazon S3 Object Storage, Bucket Policies & CloudFront CDN","Amazon RDS Managed MySQL Databases & Cloud Production Deployment"]},{"curriculum":{"exercises":[{"scenario":"Write a GitHub Actions snippet where job \"deploy\" runs only if both jobs \"frontend-test\" and \"backend-test\" pass successfully.","solution":"jobs:\n  frontend-test:\n    runs-on: ubuntu-latest\n  backend-test:\n    runs-on: ubuntu-latest\n  deploy:\n    needs: [frontend-test, backend-test]\n    runs-on: ubuntu-latest","starter_code":"jobs:\n  frontend-test:\n    runs-on: ubuntu-latest\n  backend-test:\n    runs-on: ubuntu-latest\n  deploy:\n    # TODO: add dependency condition","title":"Exercise 1: Job Dependency Chaining Syntax"},{"scenario":"Write a step in a GitHub Action that passes an encrypted secret DATABASE_PASSWORD to a database migration command.","solution":"      - name: Run Migrations\n        env:\n          DATABASE_PASSWORD: ${{ secrets.PROD_DB_PASSWORD }}\n        run: flask db upgrade","starter_code":"      - name: Run Migrations\n        # TODO: pass secret environment variable\n        run: flask db upgrade","title":"Exercise 2: Secret Environment Variable Injection"}],"objectives":["Automate the software delivery lifecycle from git commit to verified cloud release.","Write automated GitHub Actions workflows with event triggers, matrix test jobs, and dependencies.","Integrate automated linting, unit test execution, and multi-stage Docker container image builds.","Securely manage production deployment secrets and implement automated zero-downtime releases."],"outcome":"Ability to automate the software delivery lifecycle from git commit to verified cloud release.","project":"Production Multi-Stage GitHub Actions CI/CD Pipeline Automating Testing, Docker Builds & Cloud Deploy","quiz":[{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"In Continuous Delivery, software is automatically built and tested to ensure it is always ready to deploy, but the actual production release requires a manual trigger. In Continuous Deployment, every change that passes tests is deployed automatically.","id":1,"options":["Continuous Delivery is for mobile apps; Continuous Deployment is for web apps","In Continuous Delivery, releasing to production requires manual human approval, whereas in Continuous Deployment every passing build is released automatically without human intervention","Continuous Deployment does not run automated tests","Continuous Delivery uses Jenkins; Continuous Deployment uses GitHub Actions"],"question":"What is the primary difference between Continuous Delivery and Continuous Deployment?"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"In GitHub Actions, the needs keyword specifies prerequisite jobs (e.g. needs: [lint, test]). By default, jobs run in parallel unless chained with needs.","id":2,"options":["depends_on","needs","after","requires"],"question":"Which keyword in GitHub Actions workflow syntax specifies that a job must wait for another job to complete successfully before starting?"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"GitHub Actions Secrets securely encrypts sensitive values. Secret values are never exposed in logs (automatically masked) and can only be decrypted by runners during workflow execution.","id":3,"options":["In a public config.json file in the repository root","In encrypted GitHub Actions Secrets (Settings -> Secrets and variables -> Actions)","In commit messages","In the README.md file"],"question":"Where should sensitive cloud credentials (such as AWS keys or database passwords) be stored in a GitHub repository?"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Caching the package-lock dependencies avoids downloading hundreds of megabytes over the network on every commit, speeding up CI builds and conserving runner minutes.","id":4,"options":["It prevents npm from charging credit card fees","It reuses downloaded node_modules packages between runs, reducing pipeline execution time from minutes to seconds","It skips executing JavaScript unit tests","It runs tests on older Node.js versions"],"question":"What is the benefit of caching npm dependencies in GitHub Actions using actions/setup-node cache: \"npm\"?"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"By default, any step that exits with a non-zero exit code marks the job as failed and immediately terminates further step execution, preventing broken builds or failing tests from proceeding to deployment.","id":5,"options":["The runner continues executing all remaining steps as if nothing happened","The job fails immediately, stops executing subsequent steps, and marks the workflow run as failed","The workflow automatically creates a pull request fixing the error","The repository is deleted"],"question":"What happens if any single step inside a GitHub Actions job fails with an exit code other than 0?"}],"resources":[{"description":"Complete reference manual for GitHub Actions workflow syntax, triggers, and runners.","name":"GitHub Actions Official Documentation","url":"https://docs.github.com/en/actions"},{"description":"Principles and practices of automated software release management by Jez Humble.","name":"Continuous Delivery Handbook","url":"https://continuousdelivery.com/"},{"description":"Security guide on protecting pipeline secrets and dependencies.","name":"OWASP CI/CD Security Top 10","url":"https://owasp.org/www-project-top-10-ci-cd-security-risks/"}],"syllabus":[{"code_snippet":"// Typical CI/CD Pipeline Lifecycle:\n// 1. Developer pushes commit to branch 'feature/resume-parser'\n// 2. GitHub Actions fires 'pull_request' trigger:\n//    - Stage 1: ESLint & Flake8 syntax checks\n//    - Stage 2: PyTest & Jest automated test suites\n//    - Stage 3: Build Docker container image\n// 3. PR approved & merged into 'main':\n//    - Stage 4: Push tagged image to Docker Hub / AWS ECR\n//    - Stage 5: Deploy container to production cloud server","deep_dive":"Continuous Integration validates every commit with automated builds and test suites. Continuous Delivery keeps the artifact in a deployable state; Continuous Deployment automatically releases passing artifacts directly to production.","key_concepts":["Continuous Integration vs Delivery vs Deployment","Shift-Left Testing","Trunk-Based Development","Pipeline Stages (Lint -> Test -> Build -> Deploy)"],"module_title":"Module 1: CI/CD Fundamentals & Pipeline Architecture"},{"code_snippet":"# .github/workflows/ci.yml\nname: CareerSense CI Pipeline\n\non:\n  push:\n    branches: [ main ]\n  pull_request:\n    branches: [ main ]\n\njobs:\n  test:\n    runs-on: ubuntu-latest\n    steps:\n      - name: Checkout Repository\n        uses: actions/checkout@v4\n\n      - name: Setup Node.js Environment\n        uses: actions/setup-node@v4\n        with:\n          node-version: 20\n          cache: 'npm'\n          cache-dependency-path: frontend/package-lock.json\n\n      - name: Install Frontend Dependencies\n        run: cd frontend && npm ci\n\n      - name: Run Frontend Lint & Build\n        run: cd frontend && npm run build","deep_dive":"Workflows are declared in YAML. Each workflow contains one or more jobs that run in parallel by default on isolated VM runners (e.g. ubuntu-latest). The needs keyword chains jobs sequentially.","key_concepts":[".github/workflows/ YAML files","Events (push, pull_request, workflow_dispatch)","Jobs & Dependency Chaining (needs: ...)","Actions Marketplace & actions/checkout"],"module_title":"Module 2: GitHub Actions Workflow Syntax & Runners"},{"code_snippet":"# Docker Build and Publish Job in GitHub Actions\n  build-and-push:\n    needs: test\n    runs-on: ubuntu-latest\n    if: github.ref == 'refs/heads/main'\n    steps:\n      - uses: actions/checkout@v4\n      - name: Login to Docker Hub\n        uses: docker/login-action@v3\n        with:\n          username: ${{ secrets.DOCKERHUB_USERNAME }}\n          password: ${{ secrets.DOCKERHUB_TOKEN }}\n\n      - name: Build and Push Docker Image\n        uses: docker/build-push-action@v5\n        with:\n          context: ./backend\n          push: true\n          tags: ${{ secrets.DOCKERHUB_USERNAME }}/careersense-api:latest","deep_dive":"Never store credentials in code or YAML. GitHub Secrets encrypts environment variables (DOCKERHUB_USERNAME, DOCKERHUB_TOKEN). Docker Buildx utilizes GitHub cache layers to speed up builds by 80%.","key_concepts":["Container Registries (Docker Hub / GitHub GHCR)","Semantic Versioning Tags (v1.0.0, sha-...)","GitHub Secrets Vault","Docker Buildx Caching"],"module_title":"Module 3: Docker Registry Publishing & Image Tagging"},{"code_snippet":"# Deploy Step via SSH to Cloud Server\n  deploy:\n    needs: build-and-push\n    runs-on: ubuntu-latest\n    steps:\n      - name: Execute Remote SSH Deployment\n        uses: appleboy/ssh-action@v1.0.3\n        with:\n          host: ${{ secrets.EC2_HOST }}\n          username: ubuntu\n          key: ${{ secrets.EC2_SSH_PRIVATE_KEY }}\n          script: |\n            cd /opt/careersense\n            docker compose pull backend\n            docker compose up -d --no-deps backend\n            docker system prune -f","deep_dive":"Once a verified container is pushed to the registry, the deployment step SSHs into the production EC2 instance, pulls the latest image, runs database migrations, and performs a graceful rolling restart.","key_concepts":["SSH Remote Dispatch with appleboy/ssh-action","Docker Compose Pull & Up","Zero-Downtime Rolling Restarts","Rollback Strategies on Failure"],"module_title":"Module 4: Automated Cloud Deployment & Zero-Downtime Rollouts"}],"title":"Continuous Integration & Continuous Deployment Pipelines","topics":["CI/CD Core Philosophies & Branching Strategies","GitHub Actions Workflow Syntax, Runners & Triggers","Automated Unit/Integration Testing & Docker Image Registries","Secrets Management, SSH Deployment & Production Release Automation"]},"duration_weeks":"Week 4 (Sprint)","expected_outcome":"Ability to automate the software delivery lifecycle from git commit to verified cloud release.","id":569,"order_index":7,"phase_number":7,"practice_project":"Production Multi-Stage GitHub Actions CI/CD Pipeline Automating Testing, Docker Builds & Cloud Deploy","priority